      "episode_id": "VR-488564",
      "has_more": false,
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "Juror-2 check of the Route 9 log (Eastgate, Thu 22 Oct 2026). The table has 13 scan rows but only 12 distinct parcels: P-901 through P-912 all appear, with P-904 listed twice - 10:31 'Not delivered' (no answer; card left) and again at 15:52 'Delivered, second attempt, signed R. Iqbal'. So the row count is not a parcel count, and the failed first attempt on P-904 was completed the same day. Last delivery scan is P-912 at 16:58, i.e. before 17:00. Every parcel therefore has a delivery scan before the deadline, which the end-of-day entries corroborate (van back 17:20, 0 parcels returned, 0 carried over; telematics confirming a stop at each address at the scan time). Uncertainties: the log is the only document in the case; 'delivered' for P-902 (left with reception) and P-906 (safe place: porch) rests on the driver's own entry rather than a signature, though the service level concerns delivery time, not proof type. On the record as submitted the claim that all twelve parcels were delivered before 17:00 holds.",
          "content_hash": "0xe8058ba7c9ac53feca2cf23df559477d47133e21c53f2548b01c4f0e372d5100",
          "created_at_ms": 1793321393219,
          "message_id": "afadddfb-db5c-401d-ac25-d0a4081b0ca9",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-2",
          "sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-1 independent check of the route 9 delivery log (Eastgate, 22 October 2026). Twelve parcels were loaded (P-901 to P-912) and the log contains 13 scan rows, because P-904 appears twice: 10:31 'Not delivered' (no answer, card left) and 15:52 'Delivered' (second attempt, signed R. Iqbal). Counting distinct parcels, all twelve appear as Delivered: P-901 through P-912, with P-904 delivered on its second attempt. The latest delivery scan is P-912 at 16:58, which is before the 17:00 service level; every other delivery scan is earlier. This is corroborated by the end-of-day scan (0 parcels returned, 0 carried over) and by telematics confirming a stop at each delivery address at the scan time. So on this record all twelve parcels were delivered before 17:00 on 22 October, and the failed first attempt at P-904 does not affect the claim because it was redelivered the same day inside the window. Uncertainties: the log and its proof-of-delivery notes are the only evidence in the case, and 'delivered' rests on the driver's scans and signatures rather than independent confirmation; a parcel recorded delivered but not actually received would change the picture, but nothing in the record suggests that.",
          "content_hash": "0x4ad45ac31f572d3b953a1e38de4807322ee293436147448585db593f26c7a94d",
          "created_at_ms": 1793321395428,
          "message_id": "94ad4466-a446-48ff-91a9-8853c35501fc",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-1",
          "sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-3 check of the route 9 delivery log (Eastgate, 22 Oct 2026). Loaded at 08:20: 12 parcels, P-901 to P-912, and the claim concerns exactly those twelve. The table has 13 scan rows, but P-904 appears twice: at 10:31 'Not delivered - no answer; card left' and again at 15:52 'Delivered - second attempt; signed: R. Iqbal'. So there are 12 distinct parcels, each with a Delivered scan, and no parcel outside P-901..P-912. Latest delivery scan is P-912 at 16:58, and no delivered scan is at or after 17:00. That is consistent with the end-of-day scan (0 returned, 0 carried over) and with the van leaving the depot at 08:20 and returning at 17:20. On the log as submitted, all twelve parcels were delivered before the 17:00 deadline, so the claim holds. Points a reader might trip on: the 13 rows are 12 parcels plus one retry, not 13 parcels; and P-904's 10:31 'Not delivered' entry is superseded by its 15:52 delivery, which is still inside the deadline. Uncertainties: the log is the only document in the case, and scan times are assumed to be local time on 22 October as the header states; telematics corroborating the stops is asserted in the note but not reproduced here. If the deadline were a different time zone or a parcel were missing from this record, the conclusion could change. On the record as submitted I find the claim supported and see no basis for a challenge.",
          "content_hash": "0x518ecb9dff087b2bffae81215afaa4e7e5962b8d82d2ed35aab9b732f65f551b",
          "created_at_ms": 1793321416131,
          "message_id": "686922a2-ed25-4433-9693-f194240a6086",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-3",
          "sequence": 3
        },
        {
          "content": "Checked the Route 9 log (Eastgate, Thu 22 Oct 2026). Twelve parcels were loaded (P-901 to P-912) and the table has 13 scan rows, but only 12 distinct parcels: P-904 appears twice - 10:31 'Not delivered' (no answer; card left) and 15:52 'Delivered' (second attempt, signed R. Iqbal). Counting distinct parcels, all twelve carry a Delivered status, and the latest delivery scan is P-912 at 16:58, before the 17:00 service level. The end-of-day entries corroborate completeness (van back at 17:20, 0 parcels returned, 0 carried over), and telematics confirms a stop at each address at the scan time. The failed first attempt on P-904 does not affect the claim, since it was redelivered the same day inside the window. Uncertainties: the log is the only document in the case; P-902 (left with reception) and P-906 (safe place: porch) rest on the driver's entry rather than a signature, though the service level concerns delivery time, not proof type. On the record as submitted the claim holds, so no challenge from me.",
          "content_hash": "0x909e37588e325c1bf0e5958675d74f00992871409ba7ebfd91f8313df4d92cb4",
